From: jbaker@halcyon.com (James Baker)
Date: Tue, 13 Sep 1994 17:20:53 -0800
Subject: [#94-9-116] FAQ: two new questions to work on

Marvin Minsky writes, regarding memetics and evolution:
>
>I like Dawkins' conception of memes--that is, of structured units of
>knowledge that are able, more or less, to reproduce by making copies of
>themselves from one mind to another. A few million years ago, some of our
>ancestors evolved some brain machinery that was specialized for
>representing knowledge in a serial and "explicit" fashion, rather than in a
>parallel and 'implicit' manner.  Once those animals became able to transmit
>the fruits of their experience by using vocal signals, this must have led
>to rapid advances both in our existing abilities to learn and  in the
>social evolution of new ideas. By improving each brain's ability to do
>serial processing, the entire society was enabled to accumulate knowledge
>in parallel.  Of course, it could also accumulate dreadful bugs.

paragraph deleted for brevity

>However, the interaction of meme-propagation with Darwinian evolution can
>also given rise to new orders of things.  

I suspect this is not a suddenly emerging situation but more a way of
looking at things. 

For example the speech-meme mentioned above probably helped our strain take
over the niche once shared with other humanoids such as neanderthals.
Similarly the fire, agriculture, money system, navigation, and machine
building memes have lead to an increased human population and decreases in
the population of some other critters.

I think of the seperation between genetic evolution and memetic evolution
as arbitrary. It depends on how far back we choose to take credit or blame
for the results of our choices. Or how far back to define "ourselves". 

A far more interesting question to me is charting the course of future
evolution.  Humanity seems to be following the old evolutionary dictum of
"multiply" with a kind of cultural memetic inertia. I think people continue
to reproduce out of habit and for lack of a better goal. Having a new
generation defers the problem of what to do instead. 

(Confession/disclaimer: Despite my sig I may well father children myself).

So that's where we come in. What would you write as evolution's new theme?
Ideas?




